title: Keybindings
tags: GUI
kind: overview
aliases: keybinding keys shortcuts shortcut bindings sequence combo
summary: A key runs a command: every binding is a line of one file, restricted if need be to a panel type, to one text field, or to when nothing is being typed.

A ''keybinding'' maps a key — or a sequence of keys — to a command invocation,
arguments included: `ctrl+t` to `workspace:new`, `t l` to
`panel:set type metarecord-list`. Nothing else is behind a key, so whatever a key
does can also be typed in [[the command input|The command input]], clicked or sent
by a script ([[Commands]]).

All of them are in one file, `gui/keybindings.toml`, which can be edited
([[Changing keybindings]], [[The keybindings file]]). [[Keyboard navigation]] gives
the keys as they are shipped, and <<key "help:key" "h k">> tells what a key runs
([[In-app help]]).

!! A key

A ''combo'' is a key name, with its modifiers in front: `ctrl+k`, `alt+x`,
`ctrl+shift+z`. The modifiers are `ctrl`, `alt`, `shift` and `meta`, in any order,
and case does not matter.

* A character key is named by the character it produces: `a`, `/`, `:`, `[`.
* The other keys have a name: `enter`, `escape`, `backspace`, `delete`, `tab`, `space`, `up`, `down`, `left`, `right`, `home`, `end`, `pageup`, `pagedown`, `f1`…`f12`.
* The `+` key is written `plus`, since `+` joins the parts of a combo.

`shift` is written only where the key does not already say it. The character a
shifted key produces is the key's name — `:`, not `shift+;` — so `shift` appears
with a named key (`shift+enter`), with another modifier (`ctrl+shift+z`), and with a
letter (`shift+f`), whose two cases would otherwise be the same key.

A ''sequence'' is several combos typed one after the other, written with spaces:
`g r`, `m m s`. The shipped keys use it to group related commands under a first
letter: `t` then a letter for a panel type, `g` to go somewhere, `h` for help.

* Once the beginning of a sequence is typed, the GUI waits for the rest, with no time limit, and lists the possible continuations just above the status bar ([[Status bar and task bar]]).
* `Escape` gives the sequence up.
* A key that continues nothing gives it up too, and is ''not'' run on its own: the status bar says `'g x' is undefined`.
* A key that runs a command on its own never starts a sequence: with `g` bound, `g r` could not be typed where `g` applies.

!! Where a binding applies

A binding with nothing else applies everywhere, except while typing in a text
field. Three settings change that:

|!Setting |!Effect |
|`when = "<panel type>"` |only while a panel of that type has the focus — which is what lets `j` move the cursor in each list panel, with a different command in each |
|`text-input = true` |also while typing in a text field. Without it the key is left to the field: a binding on `n` must not fire in the middle of a word |
|`focus = "<name>"` |only while one particular text field has the focus, and then ''even though'' it is a text field. Two fields have a name: `find`, the find bar ([[Find in a panel]]), and `finder`, the finder of `metarecord-list` |

`when` may be combined with either of the other two. A text field is anything typed
or chosen in: an input, a text area, a drop-down list — whose arrow keys must keep
choosing in the list.

When the same key has several bindings that apply, the most specific wins: one with
`focus`, then one with `when`, then a general one; and between two of these, the one
without `text-input` — the stricter. That is how `Enter` steps through the matches
in the find bar, applies the finder in its field, opens the row in the list, and
confirms the command input everywhere else.

One key can have only one binding in a given place: the same key, `when` and `focus`.

!! What does not go through the bindings

A few keys are taken before the bindings are looked at:

* an open context menu has the keyboard to itself ([[Context menus]]);
* `Escape` first cancels a help gesture ([[In-app help]]), then leaves the full-screen mode ([[Panel slots and layout]]);
* while a script asks a question, the keys it awaits go to the script, unless a text field has the focus.

And a text field keeps its own keys: the command input's list and completion
([[The command input]]), and `ctrl+p`, `ctrl+n`, `ctrl+r` of [[Input history]].

!! Keys suggested by a panel or a user command

A panel type that is not shipped with the GUI, and the user's `gui/commands.js` ([[User commands]]),
can ''suggest'' a key for their commands (`metafolder.addKeybinding`). A suggestion
applies only where the file has no binding for the same key in the same place: the
file always wins. Suggestions are not written to the file. The built-in panels
suggest nothing — all their keys are in the file, where they can be seen and
changed.

